  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper troughing lingering over the upper OH River valley\ncombined with cold advection over warm Lake Erie waters is\nenough to produced localized, brief showers with a low\nprobability for thunderstorms through this evening. While SBCAPE\npeaks around 1000 J/kg around the I-80 corridor (enough to\nfoster deeper convection), such lackluster shear (struggling to\ntouch 20kts) should limit enough organization/intensification \nto keep storms below severe thresholds.\n\nActivity generally comes to an end after sunset as subsidence\nand loss of heating suppresses activity. The exception is\nnear/north of I-80 in northwest PA where an embedded shortwaves\nin NW could create short periods where showers (mainly) through\nMonday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLingering cool advection within northwest flow results in\nhigh temperatures around 10 degrees below the daily average\nMonday while high pressure supports dry weather (save for a low\nprobability shower near Lake Erie).\n\nHeight rises over the surface high ensures dry weather persists\nTuesday into Wednesday while elevating temperature back to near\nthe seasonal averages. Ensembles remain firm in an upper \nshortwave digging through the Ohio River Valley Thursday into \nFriday, creating the next round of widespread shower and\nthunderstorm chances. Model differences in timing of upper \nshortwave and surface boundary movement leaves large variability\nin potential storm environments (ex: spread between 25th-75th\npercentile SBCAPE values Thursday afternoon is around 700 J/kg,\na significant value). Trough passage within the summer season\nmeans this period should be monitored, but little more can be\nconfidently said at this time.",
